  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/missouri-valley-grocery-warehouse/409-mdn-produce-bldg-2009.jpg" alt="Missouri Valley Grocery Warehouse in 2009">
    <figcaption>Missouri Valley Grocery Warehouse in 2009</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Constructed as a food warehouse in 1917, the three-story, 24,000-square-foot building served as a wholesale distribution point for western North Dakota communities along the Northern Pacific Railway. These included New Salem, Glen Ullin, Hebron, Richardton, Flasher, Elgin, New Leipzig, Mott, Hazen, and Beulah.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/missouri-valley-grocery-warehouse/255-downtwn-railyd-excerpt-mar24.jpg" alt="Downtown Mandan rail yard and the site of the first Northern Pacific passenger depot">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The warehouse was built on the site of the {% place "northern-pacific-first-passenger-depot" %}Northern Pacific Railroad's first passenger depot{% endplace %}, on the southern side of Main Street at Collins Avenue. The wooden depot had been expanded and converted into the railroad's first freight house after the {% place "northern-pacific-queen-anne-depot" %}Queen Anne–style passenger depot{% endplace %} opened four blocks west in November 1882.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/missouri-valley-grocery-warehouse/180-1910-mar-8-fh-fire-cropped.jpg" alt="Northern Pacific freight house fire on March 8, 1910">
    <figcaption>The Northern Pacific freight house fire, March 8, 1910</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The earlier freight house burned to the ground in a spectacular fire on March 8, 1910. When the Northern Pacific decided to rebuild on West Main Street, away from frequent spring flooding, a business group headed by {% person "hiram-rogers-lyon" %}Hiram Lyon{% endperson %} purchased the former depot lot. The site was adjacent to the group's lumber, grain-elevator, and farm-implement businesses to the west and stood across Main Street from Lyon's First National Bank.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/missouri-valley-grocery-warehouse/708-missouri-valley-grocery-letterhead.jpg" alt="Missouri Valley Grocery Company letterhead">
    <figcaption>Missouri Valley Grocery Company letterhead</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The new warehouse stood in the heart of the commercial district and had access to an existing rail spur. The Missouri Valley Grocery Company used the building through World War II and into the late 1940s. As the trucking industry grew and offered more flexible transportation for fresh produce, the importance of rail distribution declined.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/missouri-valley-grocery-warehouse/708-downtown-aerial-sep1922.jpg" alt="Aerial view of downtown Mandan in September 1922">
    <figcaption>Downtown Mandan in September 1922; the Missouri Valley Grocery Warehouse is at lower right</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The John Iverson Company purchased the building in 1953 and used it as an electrical- and lighting-supply warehouse and wholesale-distribution center until closing its operation in 1992.</p>

<p>Several attempts were made to return the building to active use. A 2008 feasibility study proposed several ways to strengthen downtown commercial activity, including a children's museum. In 2010, another proposal envisioned a nonprofit cooperative containing small shops, an art gallery, and a Pride of Dakota store.</p>

<p>In February 2012, owner {% person "al-leingang" %}Al Leingang{% endperson %} announced a partnership with {% person "loran-galpin" %}Loran Galpin{% endperson %} of Galpin Company Inc. to renovate the building as Plaza Square on Main. The city granted property-tax exemptions and tax-increment financing totaling $735,000 over 15 years. The project was also expected to qualify for $20,000 in state tax savings.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/missouri-valley-grocery-warehouse/444-plaza-square.jpg" alt="Plaza Square on Main after renovation">
    <figcaption>Plaza Square on Main</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>All three floors were renovated for offices or mixed office and residential use, with two floors included in the project's first phase. A large glass-enclosed elevator and stair tower was added to the eastern side, with a courtyard in front. The northern and western sides were largely retained for their historic value. Concrete pillars and a scale from the building's years as a food warehouse also remained.</p>

<p>Galpin estimated a $2.1 million investment in the building's exterior and a total cost of $3.3 million to $3.6 million after the second- and third-floor renovations. The adjacent 1.2-acre lot to the east was converted into paved parking. A bank now occupies the building, with leased office space on the upper floors. The top floor was finished and the roof remodeled in 2023.</p>
